[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch_server_access
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF route advertisement for the server VLAN (10.200.0.0/24) is missing from the routing domain.

Mechanism: Both user pods send traffic for 10.200.0.x to their default gateways (10.1.1.1 and 10.2.1.1). Those gateways lack any route to 10.200.0.0/24 and immediately return ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable” (seen from host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1). Pod-to-pod traffic still works because OSPF between the access/distribution/core for 10.1.1.0/24 and 10.2.1.0/24 remains healthy. Inside the data center, servers can ping each other and their gateway because they share the same local subnet.

Localization: The server gateway is on switch_server_access br0 (10.200.0.1/24; uplink 172.16.0.11/31 on eth5). This device is not running OSPF: “ospfd is not running,” and its FRR running config is empty. Consequently, none of the OSPF speakers (router_core_1/2/3, switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1) have any route to 10.200.0.0/24 (confirmed by show ip route outputs). User-host pings to 10.200.0.2 fail with net-unreachable sourced by 10.1.1.1/10.2.1.1.

Root cause: OSPF is disabled or unconfigured on switch_server_access, so 10.200.0.0/24 is not being advertised upstream.

Corrective fix: Enable and configure OSPF on switch_server_access to advertise the server VLAN and its uplink (e.g., add 10.200.0.0/24 on br0 and 172.16.0.10/31 on eth5 to the correct OSPF area, or redistribute connected). Verify adjacency to router_core_3 forms and that 10.200.0.0/24 appears in the distribution/core routing tables. As an interim workaround, install static routes to 10.200.0.0/24 on the cores via 172.16.0.11. 
=== END ===